\begin{abstract}
The Euler--Mascheroni constant $\gam = \lim_{n\to\infty}(H_n - \ln n) = 0.5772156649\ldots$ is among the most ubiquitous constants in mathematics, yet whether it is irrational remains a famous open problem. We present a self-contained, rigorously verified development of several complementary representations of $\gam$ together with explicit quantitative control. First, we establish a \emph{positive-term series} $\gam = \sum_{k\ge 0}\big(\tfrac{1}{k+1} - \ln\tfrac{k+2}{k+1}\big)$ whose $n$-th partial sum is \emph{exactly} the lower approximant $H_n - \ln(n+1)$, yielding strict monotonic convergence and a genuine summability certificate. Second, we recast each term as a unit-interval integral, producing the classical \emph{integral representation} $\gam = \int_1^\infty\big(\tfrac{1}{\lfloor x\rfloor} - \tfrac1x\big)\,dx$. Third, we prove an \emph{effective error bound}: for all $n\ge 1$, $0 < \gam - (H_n - \ln(n+1)) < \ln(n+1)-\ln n < 1/n$, with a matching two-sided bracket. Fourth, we formalize the \emph{Stieltjes constants} $\gam_m$ and prove that the zeroth one equals $\gam$, anchoring the entire hierarchy. Finally, we develop the abstract \emph{Diophantine irrationality engine} --- a real number is irrational iff it admits arbitrarily small nonzero integer linear forms --- and specialize it to $\gam$, making precise exactly what an irrationality proof must produce and diagnosing why the known logarithmic approximants do not suffice. All results have been mechanically verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Euler introduced the constant now bearing his and Mascheroni's name in 1734, defining it as the limiting gap between the harmonic numbers and the natural logarithm:
\[
\gam := \lim_{n\to\infty}\left(H_n - \ln n\right), \qquad H_n = \sum_{k=1}^n \frac1k.
\]
The constant pervades analytic number theory (as the constant term in the Laurent expansion of $\zeta$ at $s=1$, and in Mertens' theorems), special-function theory (the digamma function satisfies $\psi(1) = -\gam$), probability, physics, and the analysis of algorithms. Despite its centrality, the arithmetic nature of $\gam$ is unknown: it is not known to be irrational, let alone transcendental.

This paper does not resolve that question. Instead it provides a clean, fully verified toolkit around $\gam$ with three goals: (i) to convert the \emph{limit} definition into honest convergent representations (a positive series and an integral) carrying constructive certificates; (ii) to make the convergence \emph{effective} with explicit, monotone, two-sided rational-rate error bounds; and (iii) to situate $\gam$ both within the Stieltjes hierarchy and within a precise Diophantine framework that states exactly what an irrationality proof of $\gam$ must establish --- and why the natural approximants fall short.

Throughout, $\N = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$, $\ln$ is the natural logarithm with the convention $\ln 0 = 0$ used by the underlying library, $H_n$ is the $n$-th harmonic number with $H_0 = 0$, and $\lfloor\cdot\rfloor$ is the floor function.

\subsection{Context and motivation}

Three features make $\gam$ unusually resistant to elementary analysis, and they motivate the structure of this paper.

First, $\gam$ is defined as a \emph{difference of two divergent quantities}. Both $H_n$ and $\ln n$ tend to $+\infty$; only their difference converges. Any estimate must therefore manage a delicate cancellation, and a naive evaluation accumulates catastrophic round-off. Recasting $\gam$ as a sum of manifestly positive terms (Section 3) or as a positive-integrand integral (Section 4) removes the cancellation entirely.

Second, the natural approximants converge \emph{slowly}, at rate exactly $\Theta(1/n)$ (Section 5). This contrasts with $e$ (super-geometric Taylor sums) and $\zeta(3)$ (Ap\'ery's 1978 argument uses geometrically convergent approximants). The rate of rational approximation is precisely what irrationality proofs exploit.

Third, and most decisively, the known approximants $\seq(n) = H_n - \ln(n+1)$ and $\seq'(n) = H_n - \ln n$ are \emph{not rational}: each carries a transcendental logarithm. An irrationality proof needs integer/rational data of controlled denominator (Section 7), which the logarithmic approximants do not supply. This is the structural heart of why $\gam$'s irrationality is open.

\section{Preliminaries}

We work with two standard one-sided approximants to $\gam$:
\[
\seq(n) := H_n - \ln(n+1), \qquad \seq'(n) := H_n - \ln n \ \ (n\ge 1).
\]
We take as given the following classical facts:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{(P1) Two-sided trapping:} for every $n$, $\ \seq(n) < \gam < \seq'(n)$.
  \item \textbf{(P2) Convergence:} $\seq(n) \to \gam$ and $\seq'(n) \to \gam$ as $n\to\infty$.
  \item \textbf{(P3) Tangent-line bound:} for all $x>0$ with $x\ne 1$, $\ \ln x < x - 1$ (strict), and for all $x>0$, $\ln x \le x-1$.
\end{itemize}
Fact (P3) is the workhorse convexity inequality; (P1)--(P2) provide the analytic backbone we upgrade into effective and structural statements.

\section{A positive-term series representation}

\begin{definition}[Series term]
For $k\in\N$,
\[
g_k := \frac{1}{k+1} - \big(\ln(k+2) - \ln(k+1)\big) = \frac{1}{k+1} - \ln\!\left(1+\frac{1}{k+1}\right).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Strict positivity; \texttt{gterm\_pos}]
For every $k\in\N$, $g_k > 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $g_k = \tfrac{1}{k+1} - \ln\frac{k+2}{k+1}$. Apply the strict tangent-line bound (P3) at $x = \frac{k+2}{k+1} > 0$, $x\ne 1$: $\ln\frac{k+2}{k+1} < \frac{k+2}{k+1} - 1 = \frac{1}{k+1}$. Rearranging gives $g_k > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Telescoping partial sum; \texttt{gterm\_partial}]
For every $n\in\N$,
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} g_k = H_n - \ln(n+1) = \seq(n).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$. The base case $n=0$ is $0=0$. For the step, $\sum_{k=0}^{n} g_k = \seq(n) + g_n$; using $H_{n+1} = H_n + \tfrac{1}{n+1}$ and $\ln(n+2)-\ln(n+1)$ from $g_n$, the logarithms recombine to $\ln(n+2)$ and the reciprocals to $H_{n+1}$, giving $\seq(n+1)$. Equivalently the logarithmic differences telescope: $\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\big(\ln(k+2)-\ln(k+1)\big) = \ln(n+1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Series representation; \texttt{hasSum\_gterm}]
The series $\sum_k g_k$ converges to $\gam$:
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} g_k = \gam, \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad \gam = \sum_{k=0}^\infty\left(\frac{1}{k+1} - \ln\frac{k+2}{k+1}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By positivity the terms are nonnegative, and every partial sum equals $\seq(n)$, which by (P1) is bounded above by $\gam$. Hence the partial sums are nondecreasing and bounded, so $\sum_k g_k$ is summable. Its sum is the limit of its partial sums; but those equal $\seq(n)$, which by (P2) tends to $\gam$. Uniqueness of limits identifies the sum as $\gam$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict monotonicity; \texttt{strictMono\_eulerMascheroniSeq}]
$\seq$ is strictly increasing: $\seq(n) < \seq(n+1)$ for all $n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\seq(n+1) - \seq(n) = g_n > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact increment; \texttt{eulerMascheroniSeq\_succ\_sub}]
$\seq(n+1) - \seq(n) = g_n$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Integral representation}

\begin{lemma}[Integrand nonnegativity; \texttt{integrand\_nonneg}]
For $k\in\N$ and $x \ge k+1$, $\ \tfrac{1}{k+1} - \tfrac1x \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $0 < k+1 \le x$, monotonicity of $t\mapsto 1/t$ on positives gives $\tfrac1x \le \tfrac{1}{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Term as integral; \texttt{gterm\_eq\_integral}]
For every $k\in\N$,
\[
g_k = \int_{k+1}^{\,k+2}\left(\frac{1}{k+1} - \frac1x\right)dx.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The integrand splits into a constant and $-1/x$. On $[k+1,k+2]$ we have $0\notin[k+1,k+2]$, so $1/x$ is interval-integrable with $\int_{k+1}^{k+2}\tfrac1x\,dx = \ln(k+2)-\ln(k+1)$, while $\int_{k+1}^{k+2}\tfrac{1}{k+1}\,dx = \tfrac{1}{k+1}$. Subtracting gives exactly $g_k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Integral representation; \texttt{hasSum\_integral\_repr}]
\[
\gam = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\int_{k+1}^{\,k+2}\left(\frac{1}{k+1}-\frac1x\right)dx = \int_{1}^{\infty}\left(\frac{1}{\lfloor x\rfloor}-\frac1x\right)dx.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substitute the previous theorem termwise into the series representation. Each summand is the integral over the unit window $[k+1,k+2]$, where $\lfloor x\rfloor = k+1$, so the integrand coincides with $\tfrac{1}{\lfloor x\rfloor} - \tfrac1x$; concatenating the windows yields the improper integral over $[1,\infty)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Integral partial sums bracket $\gam$; \texttt{integral\_partialSum\_lt\_lt\_seq'}]
For every $n$,
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\int_{k+1}^{\,k+2}\left(\frac{1}{k+1}-\frac1x\right)dx \;<\; \gam \;<\; \seq'(n).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The left sum equals $\seq(n)$; apply the trapping (P1).
\end{proof}

\section{Effective error bounds}

\begin{lemma}[Bracket width; \texttt{eulerMascheroni\_trap\_width\_eq}]
For $n\ge 1$, $\ \seq'(n) - \seq(n) = \ln(n+1) - \ln n.$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct computation: $(H_n - \ln n) - (H_n - \ln(n+1)) = \ln(n+1)-\ln n$ for $n\ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Effective lower error; \texttt{eulerMascheroniConstant\_sub\_seq\_lt}]
For $n\ge 1$, $\ 0 < \gam - \seq(n) < \ln(n+1) - \ln n.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity is the left half of (P1). For the upper bound, $\gam - \seq(n) < \seq'(n) - \seq(n)$ by the right half of (P1), and the right side equals the width.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Effective upper error; \texttt{seq'\_sub\_eulerMascheroniConstant\_lt}]
For $n\ge 1$, $\ 0 < \seq'(n) - \gam < \ln(n+1) - \ln n.$
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Rational $1/n$ bound]
For $n\ge 1$,
\[
0 < \gam - \big(H_n - \ln(n+1)\big) < \frac1n, \qquad \big|\,\seq(n) - \gam\,\big| < \frac1n.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the strict tangent-line bound (P3) at $x = \tfrac{n+1}{n}$, $\ \ln(n+1)-\ln n = \ln\tfrac{n+1}{n} < \tfrac{n+1}{n} - 1 = \tfrac1n$. Combine with the effective lower error. The absolute-value form is \texttt{abs\_eulerMascheroniSeq\_sub\_lt}.
\end{proof}

\section{The Stieltjes anchor}

\begin{definition}[Stieltjes sequence; \texttt{stieltjesSeq}]
For $m,n\in\N$,
\[
S_m(n) := \sum_{k=1}^{n}\frac{(\ln k)^m}{k} - \frac{(\ln n)^{m+1}}{m+1}.
\]
The $m$-th Stieltjes constant is $\gam_m := \lim_{n\to\infty} S_m(n)$.
\end{definition}
These constants are the Laurent coefficients of the Riemann zeta function at its pole:
\[
\zeta(s) = \frac{1}{s-1} + \sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^m}{m!}\,\gam_m\,(s-1)^m.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Collapse at $m=0$; \texttt{stieltjesSeq\_zero\_eq}]
For every $n$, $\ S_0(n) = H_n - \ln n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $m=0$, $(\ln k)^0 = 1$ so the sum is $\sum_{k=1}^n \tfrac1k = H_n$, and the correction term is $\ln n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Agreement with upper approximant; \texttt{stieltjesSeq\_zero\_eq\_seq'}]
For $n\ge 1$, $\ S_0(n) = \seq'(n)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Zeroth Stieltjes constant is $\gam$; \texttt{tendsto\_stieltjesSeq\_zero}]
$\gam_0 = \lim_{n\to\infty} S_0(n) = \gam.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$S_0$ and $\seq'$ agree for all $n\ge 1$, i.e.\ eventually. Since $\seq'\to\gam$, the eventually-equal sequence $S_0$ has the same limit. The only subtlety is the corner $n=0$ (where $\ln 0 = 0$ forces $S_0(0)=0$ while $\seq'(0)$ uses a junk value); eventual equality on $n\ge 1$ is exactly what is needed.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Upper bracketing; \texttt{eulerMascheroniConstant\_lt\_stieltjesSeq\_zero}]
For $n\ge 1$, $\ \gam < S_0(n)$.
\end{proposition}

\section{A Diophantine irrationality criterion for $\gam$}

\begin{theorem}[Irrationality engine, sufficient form; \texttt{irrational\_of\_forall\_eps\_linear\_form}]
Let $x\in\R$. If for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exist $q\in\N$ with $q\ge 1$ and $p\in\Z$ such that $0 < |q x - p| < \varepsilon$, then $x$ is irrational.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Contrapositive. If $x = a/b$ with $b\ge 1$, then $qx - p = (qa - pb)/b$ is rational with denominator $b$; if nonzero, $|qx-p|\ge 1/b$. Choosing $\varepsilon = 1/b$ contradicts the hypothesis. A sequence form (\texttt{irrational\_of\_tendsto\_linear\_form}) states the same with integer forms $q_n x - p_n \ne 0$ tending to $0$ and $q_n \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Necessary form via Dirichlet; \texttt{forall\_eps\_linear\_form\_of\_irrational}]
If $x$ is irrational, then for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exist $q\ge 1$ and $p\in\Z$ with $0 < |qx-p| < \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Dirichlet's approximation theorem yields $q\ge 1$ with $|qx - \mathrm{round}(qx)| < \varepsilon$; irrationality guarantees this form is nonzero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Characterization; \texttt{irrational\_iff\_forall\_eps\_linear\_form}]
For every $x\in\R$,
\[
x\ \text{is irrational}\iff \forall \varepsilon>0,\ \exists\, q\ge 1,\ p\in\Z:\ 0 < |qx-p| < \varepsilon.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Specialization to $\gam$; \texttt{irrational\_eulerMascheroniConstant\_iff}]
\[
\gam\ \text{is irrational}\iff \forall \varepsilon>0,\ \exists\, q\ge 1,\ p\in\Z:\ 0 < |q\gam - p| < \varepsilon.
\]
\end{theorem}

We also record elementary structural facts: $0 < \gam$ (\texttt{eulerMascheroniConstant\_pos}), $\gam < 1$ (\texttt{eulerMascheroniConstant\_lt\_one}), the combined sandwich $\seq(n) < \gam < \seq'(n)$ (\texttt{eulerMascheroniSeq\_sandwich}), and that the trapping interval width tends to $0$ (\texttt{tendsto\_eulerMascheroni\_trap\_width}).

\paragraph{The structural obstruction.} The specialization states the exact Diophantine target. Yet the natural approximants $\seq(n) = H_n - \ln(n+1)$ and $\seq'(n) = H_n - \ln n$ bracket $\gam$ in an interval of width $\ln(1+1/n)\to 0$ whose endpoints are \emph{transcendental} (they carry a logarithm), not rational. They therefore do not directly feed the engine, which requires integer/rational data. This is a precise diagnosis of why $\gam$'s irrationality is hard: the obstruction is not the convergence speed per se but the absence of approximants with controlled rational denominators and width $o(1/q)$.

\section{Numerical illustration}

We record concrete values consistent with $\gam = 0.5772156649\ldots$ The first five series terms are $g_0\approx0.306853$, $g_1\approx0.094535$, $g_2\approx0.045651$, $g_3\approx0.026856$, $g_4\approx0.017678$, all positive and $\sim \tfrac{1}{2(k+1)^2}$; their cumulative sums reproduce $\seq(n)$ exactly. The effective bracket is illustrated below:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rllll}
\hline
$n$ & $\seq(n)$ & $\seq'(n)$ & $\gam-\seq(n)$ & $1/n$ \\
\hline
$10$ & $0.531073$ & $0.626383$ & $4.61\times10^{-2}$ & $1.0\times10^{-1}$ \\
$100$ & $0.572257$ & $0.582207$ & $4.96\times10^{-3}$ & $1.0\times10^{-2}$ \\
$1000$ & $0.576716$ & $0.577716$ & $5.00\times10^{-4}$ & $1.0\times10^{-3}$ \\
$10000$ & $0.577166$ & $0.577266$ & $5.00\times10^{-5}$ & $1.0\times10^{-4}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The error is consistently about half the bound $1/n$, reflecting $\gam-\seq(n)\sim\tfrac{1}{2n}$; the inequality of Corollary~5.4 holds with room to spare, and the bracket holds at every row. A windowwise midpoint rule reproduces each $g_k$ to ten digits and sums to $0.577116\ldots$ over the first $5000$ windows; the Stieltjes sequence $S_0(n)=H_n-\ln n$ decreases to $\gam$ from above ($S_0(1000)=0.577716$), confirming Proposition~6.5. These are consistency checks, not proofs.

\section{Discussion}

The development upgrades the limit definition of $\gam$ into two certified representations --- a positive monotone series and an integral over the floor-staircase --- that agree term by term; makes the convergence quantitative and two-sided with a clean $1/n$ rational bound; and places $\gam$ as the anchor $\gam_0$ of the Stieltjes hierarchy and within an exact Diophantine framework that pinpoints what an irrationality proof must construct and why current approximants fall short. None of these results settles the irrationality of $\gam$, which remains open; their value is in providing rigorous, reusable infrastructure on which sharper approximation schemes can be built and tested.

We highlight the \emph{complementarity} of the representations: the series is best for monotone lower bounds and summability; the integral connects $\gam$ to the geometry of the floor-staircase; the Stieltjes sequence embeds $\gam$ in the spectral data of $\zeta$; and the Diophantine criterion reframes the open problem as a concrete existence statement about integer linear forms. A future advance may combine them --- e.g.\ using the integral form to engineer rational approximants of controlled denominator that feed the engine of Section 7. We do not claim irrationality of $\gam$, nor an improvement of the $O(1/n)$ rate within the verified development.

\section{Future work}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Quadratic-rate acceleration.} Center the logarithm at the midpoint: conjecturally $|\gam - (H_n - \ln(n+\tfrac12))| < \tfrac{1}{24n^2}$, an $O(1/n^2)$ improvement obtained by a midpoint-quadrature cancellation of the leading $O(1/n)$ error.
  \item \textbf{Strictly interior term bounds.} Sharpen interval memberships of the soft-max / log-gap terms from closed to open, upgrading $\le$ to strict $<$.
  \item \textbf{Temperature-deformed $\gam$.} Study $\gam(c) := \sum_{k\ge1}\big(\tfrac1k - \mathrm{softMax}_c(0,-\ln k)\big)$ interpolating between log-sum-exp ($c=1$, giving $\gam$) and a hard-max tropical limit ($c\to\infty$).
  \item \textbf{Stieltjes telescoping certificates.} Extend the telescoping identity to $\gam_1$: conjecturally $\gam_1 = \sum_{k\ge1}\big(-\tfrac{\ln k}{k} + \tfrac{\ln^2(k+1)-\ln^2 k}{2}\big)$ with an $O(\ln n / n)$ effective bound.
\end{itemize}

